% EMNLP 2026 Main — ARR May 2026
% Generational Depth Contamination
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[review]{acl}
\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{subcaption}
\usepackage{placeins}

\newcommand{\Kstar}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Kempirical}{{K^*_{\text{empirical}}}}
\newcommand{\Ktheory}{{K^*_{\text{theory}}}}
\newcommand{\auc}{\text{AUC}}
\newcommand{\jsd}{\text{JSD}}

\title{Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text:\\Measuring the Discrimination Boundary}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% === Abstract ===
\begin{abstract}
Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
We measure the discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$---the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain distinguishable---using 15 statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier.
Across 4 model-mode configurations and 4 decoding strategies (${\leq}1.5$B scale), $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Cohen's $h{\approx}0.20$), invariant across all 8 conditions: a single generation step is reliably detectable, but the second renders text statistically indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content---a \emph{one-wash laundering} effect.
Model-free statistical features enable 80--92\% cross-model transfer with scorer-invariant feature ordering ($\rho > 0.90$), and cross-scorer validation confirms that $\Kempirical$ is governed by the generation process rather than the scoring model.
Both supervised classification and zero-shot frontier LLMs fail comparably at depths ${\geq}2$ (recall 14--22\% vs.\ 8--38\%), confirming $\Kempirical{=}2$ as a property of the data rather than a method limitation.
Downstream, $\Kempirical{=}2$ correctly predicts that binary curation minimizes degradation most effectively among all tested strategies.
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

% === Section 2: Related Work ===
\input{related_work}

% === Section 3: Method ===
\input{method}

% === Section 4: Experiments ===
\input{experiments_c1}

% === Section 5: K*-Aware Data Filtering ===
\input{experiments_c3}

% === Section 6: Discussion ===
\input{discussion}

% === Section 7: Conclusion ===
\input{conclusion}

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Limitations}

Our findings are bounded by (1)~model scale ($\leq$1.5B), (2)~prompt-chain rather than retrain-chain protocols (our $\Kempirical$ is a conservative upper bound on retrain-chain $\Kstar$ under monotonic-degradation assumptions), (3)~English C4 text only, (4)~four decoding strategies, (5)~threshold sensitivity ($\Kempirical$ ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$ depending on configuration; all four configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at our chosen $\theta{=}0.60$), (6)~marginal boundary (Qwen-continuation 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.0015; cross-scorer validation with both Pythia and OLMo scorers drops this condition to $\Kempirical{=}1$, Section~\ref{sec:transfer}), (7)~the 15D feature set yields a feature-set-specific lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher), (8)~the neural probe boundary is model-dependent ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$ for Qwen only; $2$ for Pythia/OLMo), likely reflecting self-familiarity rather than a universal signal, and (9)~while overall feature rankings are highly consistent across models ($\rho \geq 0.85$), depth-pair-level agreement weakens at the 1v2 boundary ($\tau \approx 0.37$), indicating that per-feature contributions at the critical boundary vary across models.
Detailed analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing---positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use the publicly available C4 corpus and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc > 0.99$ at 0v1) remains robust.

\bibliography{references}

\input{appendix}
\input{appendix_extended}

\end{document}

\section{Application: $\Kstar$-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

$\Kempirical = 2$ makes a falsifiable prediction: if depth assignments beyond $k{=}2$ carry no discriminative signal, graduated refinement beyond the binary boundary should provide no additional benefit---binary curation should suffice, and graduated performance should converge toward binary as the decay steepness increases.
We test this directly by comparing six data curation strategies on a corpus of 30{,}000 documents (5{,}000 per depth, depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter} (uniform weight), (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from the 15D LightGBM classifier), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson} (importance weighting following \citet{drayson2025}, same classifier, weights $w_j \propto (1 - q(x_j))^b$, $b{=}1$, cap $r_{\max}{=}3$), (iv)~\textbf{graduated} (exponential decay $w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain documents above corpus median surprisal), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir} (importance resampling against depth-0 reference).

We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs, evaluating on MMLU (14{,}042 examples, 0-shot) and HellaSwag (10{,}042 examples, 0-shot) across 5 seeds; the graduated strategy is further crossed with 5~$\alpha$ values (51 total runs).
This 100\% synthetic setting provides an upper-bound characterization; in realistic mixed-corpus settings, the filtering benefit scales with the contamination fraction~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, dohmatob2024model}.

\paragraph{Results.}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Downstream performance of six data filtering strategies. Fine-tuning: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, LoRA, 30K documents, 5 seeds. Graduated shows best $\alpha$ only; full sweep in Table~\ref{tab:tab_alpha_sweep}.}
\label{tab:tab_filtering_main}
\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}
\end{table}

All strategies degrade absolute performance relative to the base model (MMLU 0.6018, HellaSwag 0.6821); the goal is damage minimization, not improvement.
Binary-ours minimizes MMLU degradation most effectively (Table~\ref{tab:tab_filtering_main}), reducing the gap from $-$1.53\,pp (no-filter) to $-$0.77\,pp---an approximately 50\% reduction.
Pairwise paired $t$-tests with Holm-Bonferroni correction confirm that binary-ours significantly outperforms all five alternatives on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} < 0.05$); 8 of 15 main-strategy pairs are significant on MMLU, 9 of 15 on HellaSwag (full matrix in Table~\ref{tab:pairwise_significance}).
The strategy ordering is interpretable through $\Kempirical$: binary filtering exploits the one reliable boundary (0v1), while graduated strategies assign different weights to depths 2--5 based on near-random classifier outputs (2v3 $\auc < \theta$), effectively injecting noise into the importance weights.
DSIR performs worst, only marginally above no-filter: its bigram feature hashing captures domain shift but not the depth-correlated distributional signatures that drive depth discrimination.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_filtering_strategies.pdf}
\caption{Downstream performance of all filtering strategies (5 seeds, $\pm$1 std). Binary-ours (green) minimizes MMLU degradation; graduated $\alpha$ sweep shows monotonic convergence toward binary. Dashed line: base model (no fine-tuning).}
\label{fig:filtering_strategies}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{$\alpha$ sensitivity.}
Higher $\alpha$ values yield monotonically better performance on both benchmarks (HellaSwag: $0.6640$ at $\alpha{=}0.1$ to $0.6701$ at $\alpha{=}0.9$).
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, graduated filtering narrows the MMLU gap to 0.49\,pp below binary-ours ($0.5892$ vs.\ $0.5941$; $p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$) but matches binary-ours exactly on HellaSwag ($0.6701$; $\Delta = 0$).
The convergence at high $\alpha$ is expected: as $\alpha \to \infty$, graduated weighting degenerates to binary thresholding.
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, depth-2 text receives only 16.5\% weight ($e^{-0.9 \times 2}$), functionally approximating a binary threshold---hence the HellaSwag equivalence, while the more noise-sensitive MMLU still detects the residual difference (detailed $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).

\paragraph{Summary.}
Binary-ours significantly outperforms all alternatives on MMLU after Holm-Bonferroni correction (8/15 main-strategy pairs significant); on HellaSwag, 9/15 pairs are significant.
On HellaSwag, graduated at $\alpha{=}0.9$ matches binary-ours exactly ($\Delta = 0$, $p_{\text{corr}} = 1.00$), consistent with steep exponential decay functionally approximating binary thresholding; graduated also significantly outperforms surprisal ($p_{\text{corr}} = .002$), while DSIR is indistinguishable from no-filter on both benchmarks.
Significance is assessed within two separate families: the 6 main strategies (15 pairs, addressing ``which strategy is best?'') and the graduated $\alpha$ sweep (``optimal $\alpha$?''), as they correspond to distinct research questions.
The benchmark-specific pattern reinforces $\Kempirical{=}2$: MMLU, which is more sensitive to importance-weight noise, confirms strict dominance of binary filtering; HellaSwag shows that even near-binary graduated weighting suffices, but finer ordinal structure offers no benefit.

